This section discusses the findings of the study in relation to the research questions (see Objectives) and previous research (see Background) of this study. The findings cover the following sections: (1) Key Findings, (2) Point 1, (3) Point 2, (4) Point 3, (5) Study Limitations, and (6) Future Research. 


== Key Findings
// The medium preference was for something they re familiar with to reduce learning curve, but added requirement was it needed to be portable to support their day to day workflow. 

The accumulated results of this study's contextual inquiry and co-design sessions suggests a positive outlook on AI supported exergame-based rehabilitation. Participants found that a combination of digitalization of rehabilitation and AI, an inevitable future, would enable effective personalization and high exercise repetitions, while simultaneously appearing fun and motivating for the patients. Subsequently, reducing time consumption and effort, a common and critical constraint within the rehabilitation field, was also considered as a major key benefit. Hence, all participants emphasized that under time pressure, they would be willing to accept the AI system's therapy plan and all additional parameters even if imperfect, *but only/with condition* as long as patient safety was not compromised if the required effort would otherwise be too high. This trade-off was however only considered if the additional time and effort put, would not provide significantly better outcomes for the patient. *Some connective word* viewing AI-supported rehabilitation as an inevitable future, they hoped it would improve higher accessibility *and/through* remote rehabilitation for patients that live far or can not visit often. 

In spite of the positive attitude towards AI-supported exergame-based rehabilitation, there were conditions to the shared therapist-system agency relating to *the sense of* autonomy and control. In particular, participants preferred shared agency in the form of system recommendations which required therapists to accept, adjust or reject (Scenario 2) and, a system which requires instructions/inputs by the therapx ists (Scenario 3) as they felt more in control. As opposed to the fully autonomous system (Scenario 4) which majority of participants were strongly opposed to, perceiving a strong threat towards their professional role and expertise - despite knowing they could override the system by accepting, adjusting or rejecting as well. Only two participants were comfortable with the autonomous system, elaborating they would not feel replaced, but rather ... to use the *time and effort* on patient goals instead. Irrespective of participants individual concerns, they were overall only willing to let go of control based on the scarcity of time and level of trust in the system, something that can only be built over time and based on the results it consistently produced. The emphasis on acquired control was *argued* to assure better adaptation to each patient as every patient and day is unique. *Another concern was how overreliance on this type of system could potentially reduce clinical reasoning skills, especially for novice therapists.* // was this only for AI or for all scenarios?? 

Although there was an overall obvious preference for a system with agency in reasoning (S2) and a system with agency in execution (S3), the system requirements varied across the *four main phases of therapy*. During therapy planning & goal-setting (Phase 1), participants generally preferred higher self-agency mainly through providing instructions to the system (S3) and manual control without any system support (Scenario 1). During session planning (Phase 2) however, participants wanted less manual control, and more system with hybrid control structure where the system either *managed* agency in reasoning (S2) or agency in execution (S3). On the other hand, for the rehabilitation session (Phase 3), participants wanted to delegate the system more agency in reasoning (S2) along with some agency in execution (S4), intervening manually only when absolutely necessary. Finally, for the post-session evaluation (Phase 4), participants wanted the least amount of manual control (S1), mainly preferring agency in reasoning (S2), with some agency in execution (S3 & S4). Surprisingly, system with full human agency in reasoning in reasoning and execution (S1) was picked even less than full system agency in reasoning and execution (S4) despite the fearful thoughts of possible replacement by AI. //Mention the discrepancies were present between what they desired and what was designed.

Another factor which affected the participants sense of agency was related to details of the information available on the various systems (S1-S4). Whether the system required full human agency in reasoning and execution (S1), human agency in execution with system agency in reasoning (S2) or system agency in reasoning with human agency in execution (S3), all participants revealed a need for a lesser degree of details. Instead of fine-grained details as default, participants only wanted curated information that relate to functional diagnostic goals to sustain a natural workflow *(parameters??)* and mental-model of a therapist. Nevertheless, participants still wanted the option for more in-depth information and parameter options when needed, suggesting a preference for a balanced design which combines hierarchal importance and simplicity. //a balanced design that combines simplicity, focus on important informations, preset prompts, and manual adjustments for more technical experienced users. 
Access to additional in-depth information was also deemed important when there was uncertainty or a disagreement with the system, particularly for valuable for novice therapists. This was beneficial because it could help the therapist reflect, learn and understand why there was a difference in reasoning (S2), what changes the system made based on their instructions (S3) or deduce if they might have missed something. On the contrary, derived from the lack of trust, participants wanted a *much* higher degree of detail for the fully automated system (S4). As such, fully understanding that the system is accurately reasoning and executing the necessary *actions* correctly was perceived as crucial to assure that patients were receiving safe and qualitative rehabilitation. The only time details felt like a burden to the therapists, was when they themselves had to provide the system with detailed input (S3). 

Lastly, while AI-supported exergame-based system was advocated for, participants underscored how it still can not cover for all the necessities for a successful rehabilitation. The patient-therapist relationship specifically, was predominantly reported as something that could not be substituted with a system. The participants argued that the relationship is necessary for creating better motivation and trust, this increase the likelihood of consistent and correct rehabilitation. The weight of building a strong patient-therapist relationship, along with controlling progress, and providing and receiving feedback resulted in participants rather willing to see the patient than not seeing the patient but receiving additional quantitive data enabled through the AI-supported system. The only case where participants could consider not seeing the patient was again related to trust in the system to provide correct and effective rehabilitation without compromising ethical or security measures. 



